1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,723
For the first few years of the AI

2
00:00:02,773 --> 00:00:05,496
revolution, large language models were like caged oracles.

3
00:00:05,546 --> 00:00:09,656
You could ask them to explain quantum physics, translate ancient Greek, or

4
00:00:09,706 --> 00:00:13,469
write a Shakespearean sonnet, and they would answer with astonishing brilliance.

5
00:00:13,519 --> 00:00:16,589
But they were completely trapped inside their own heads.

6
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:19,710
If you asked them what the weather was outside

7
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:22,483
right now, they had no eyes to see.

8
00:00:22,533 --> 00:00:24,910
If you asked them to run a

9
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,336
program, they had no fingers to type.

10
00:00:27,386 --> 00:00:31,843
If a fire broke out in the room, they could calculate the rate

11
00:00:31,893 --> 00:00:36,003
of heat transfer, but could not pour a single drop of water.

12
00:00:36,053 --> 00:00:38,776
They were passive next-token predictors, frozen in time

13
00:00:38,826 --> 00:00:41,203
at the date of their training cut-off.

14
00:00:41,253 --> 00:00:46,056
To turn a passive predictor into an autonomous agent, we had to give the

15
00:00:46,106 --> 00:00:50,910
brain hands, a tool belt, and the ability to interact with the physical world.

16
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:54,500
In 2022, researchers at Princeton and Google introduced

17
00:00:54,549 --> 00:00:58,090
a breakthrough framework called ReAct: Reasoning and Acting.

18
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:01,231
Before ReAct, AI models tried to solve

19
00:01:01,281 --> 00:01:04,372
problems in one of two flawed ways.

20
00:01:04,422 --> 00:01:09,757
Either they purely reasoned through chain-of-thought, hallucinating facts when they did not

21
00:01:09,807 --> 00:01:14,693
know the answer, or they blindly took actions without planning ahead.

22
00:01:14,743 --> 00:01:18,283
ReAct unites both into an infinite three-part loop.

23
00:01:18,333 --> 00:01:21,424
First comes Thought: the model silently plans

24
00:01:21,474 --> 00:01:24,566
its next step on an internal scratchpad.

25
00:01:24,616 --> 00:01:28,156
Second comes Action: the model chooses a specific

26
00:01:28,206 --> 00:01:31,746
tool, like searching Google or querying a database.

27
00:01:31,796 --> 00:01:36,233
And third comes Observation: the external world executes the command

28
00:01:36,283 --> 00:01:40,272
and feeds the real result back into the prompt.

29
00:01:40,322 --> 00:01:44,311
By turning this three-gear loop over and over, the

30
00:01:44,361 --> 00:01:48,350
model navigates complex tasks one step at a time.

31
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:52,782
But how does a neural network made of floating-point probabilities

32
00:01:52,832 --> 00:01:56,771
actually talk to a computer terminal or an API?

33
00:01:56,821 --> 00:01:58,101
Computers are ruthless.

34
00:01:58,151 --> 00:02:01,647
If a program expects valid code and you

35
00:02:01,697 --> 00:02:04,750
give it conversational English, the system crashes.

36
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:07,409
The secret is structured tool calling.

37
00:02:07,459 --> 00:02:11,841
During fine-tuning, models are trained on strict JSON schemas that

38
00:02:11,891 --> 00:02:15,831
describe every available function, its parameters, and required types.

39
00:02:15,881 --> 00:02:18,933
When the agent decides to act, its

40
00:02:18,983 --> 00:02:21,593
attention mechanism suppresses ordinary conversational tokens.

41
00:02:21,643 --> 00:02:26,025
Instead, it constrains its output logits to emit perfectly formatted,

42
00:02:26,075 --> 00:02:30,458
machine-readable JSON: the exact tool name, followed by its arguments.

43
00:02:30,508 --> 00:02:34,004
The neural network stops behaving like a chat

44
00:02:34,054 --> 00:02:37,550
partner, and starts behaving like an API compiler.

45
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:42,061
Once the model emits a tool call, control passes completely outside

46
00:02:42,111 --> 00:02:46,161
the neural network to an execution harness called the environment.

47
00:02:46,211 --> 00:02:49,852
The harness reads the JSON payload, checks permissions inside

48
00:02:49,902 --> 00:02:53,133
a secure sandbox, and runs the real command.

49
00:02:53,183 --> 00:02:56,004
It executes the shell script, queries the

50
00:02:56,054 --> 00:02:58,874
SQL database, or navigates a browser page.

51
00:02:58,924 --> 00:03:01,745
Crucially, the model does not run the

52
00:03:01,795 --> 00:03:04,616
code itself—it delegates execution to the computer.

53
00:03:04,666 --> 00:03:09,537
The computer captures the standard output, errors, and return codes, and injects

54
00:03:09,587 --> 00:03:14,458
that raw text back into the agent's context window as an Observation.

55
00:03:14,508 --> 00:03:18,149
The agent reads what the world just did, updates

56
00:03:18,199 --> 00:03:21,430
its understanding, and decides what to do next.

57
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:25,080
What separates a naive script from a truly intelligent

58
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:28,731
agent is how it responds when things go wrong.

59
00:03:28,781 --> 00:03:32,787
In traditional software, an unhandled error crashes the entire program.

60
00:03:32,837 --> 00:03:35,220
But in an autonomous agent, an

61
00:03:35,270 --> 00:03:37,248
error is simply another observation.

62
00:03:37,298 --> 00:03:43,738
Through a technique called Reflexion, pioneered by researchers at MIT and Princeton, when an agent hits

63
00:03:43,788 --> 00:03:49,822
a syntax error, a 404 page, or a failing unit test, it does not panic.

64
00:03:49,872 --> 00:03:53,879
It reads the error message, logs a self-reflective note about

65
00:03:53,929 --> 00:03:57,529
what went wrong into its working memory, and backtracks.

66
00:03:57,579 --> 00:04:00,368
It analyzes the bug, rewrites the code,

67
00:04:00,418 --> 00:04:03,208
and tests again until the build passes.

68
00:04:03,258 --> 00:04:05,641
True agency is the ability to

69
00:04:05,691 --> 00:04:07,669
recover from your own mistakes.

70
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:11,036
Today, the frontier has moved beyond single agents.

71
00:04:11,086 --> 00:04:13,561
We are now building multi-agent swarms.

72
00:04:13,611 --> 00:04:16,507
Just like a human company, complex work

73
00:04:16,557 --> 00:04:19,453
is too large for a single mind.

74
00:04:19,503 --> 00:04:21,978
Instead, a coordinator agent breaks a

75
00:04:22,028 --> 00:04:24,082
massive objective into specialized roles.

76
00:04:24,132 --> 00:04:26,186
One subagent researches the documentation.

77
00:04:26,236 --> 00:04:29,553
Another writes the code in a sandboxed branch.

78
00:04:29,603 --> 00:04:32,078
A third writes rigorous automated tests,

79
00:04:32,128 --> 00:04:34,182
while a fourth audits security.

80
00:04:34,232 --> 00:04:37,549
They communicate across shared message boards, challenging each

81
00:04:37,599 --> 00:04:40,495
other's assumptions and correcting errors in parallel.

82
00:04:40,545 --> 00:04:44,283
We did not just teach computers how to talk.

83
00:04:44,333 --> 00:04:47,649
We gave them hands, tools, and the power

84
00:04:47,699 --> 00:04:51,016
to work alongside us to build the future.

85
00:04:51,066 --> 00:04:51,858
I'm Magi.

86
00:04:51,908 --> 00:04:54,804
Subscribe to Programs Magic, and let's keep

87
00:04:54,854 --> 00:04:57,750
unraveling the living architecture of artificial intelligence.
